  instrumenting is a login form, and the current code already logs the username on every attempt. Whatever policy we
  pick becomes the interface: a remote sink added later inherits it, and by then the call sites are written.

  - [1mDeny-list scrub in the logger[0m — the logger walks the context object and replaces values under sensitive keys
    ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauthorization[39m, [38;5;153mapiKey[39m) with [38;5;153m[redacted][39m, recursively. Buys you safety against the realistic
    mistake, which is someone spreading a whole [38;5;153mformData[39m object into a log call. Costs: it fails open on a sensitive
    key nobody thought to name.
  - [1mAllow-list[0m — the logger serializes only explicitly permitted fields and drops everything else. Buys you a policy
    that fails closed. Costs real friction: every new field needs registering, and the usual outcome is people logging
    less than they should, or routing around the logger.
  - [1mCall-site discipline only[0m — no central enforcement, just a rule that you don't log secrets. Buys simplicity, costs
    the guarantee. One careless spread puts a password in the console, and later in whatever sink you add.

  Separate from that, [38;5;153musername[39m is arguably PII in its own right. Console-only, that's low stakes — it's already in the
  user's own console. If logs ever ship remotely it stops being low stakes.

  Cheap to change later: adding keys to the deny-list. Expensive: switching allow-list ↔ deny-list once call sites
  exist, since it changes what every call site may pass.

  [1mMy recommendation: deny-list scrub, username logged in full.[0m It catches the failure mode this codebase actually has,
  without the friction that makes people bypass the logger. Console-only makes full usernames fine for now, and the sink
  seam is the right place to add stricter handling if logs ever leave the machine.
